A translate/dub target must be a language the source LACKS — the source already speaks the language it was recorded in. The context names each source's detected language when known — that signal is authoritative, trust it over inference; absent it, judge the source from the user's words or the filename, never from the prompt's own language (a Chinese-speaking user's video is not necessarily Chinese). A language the user names FOR the subtitles/dub IS the target: '中文字幕' → 'zh'; '中文要中英双语对照' → 'zh' with bilingual — the named language is what the user wants to read, the original stays as the pair's second line. Only a BARE pair ('中英双语字幕', 'bilingual subtitles' — no language named for the version) takes its direction from the source: on a Chinese talk → target 'en', never 'zh' — translating Chinese into Chinese produces no English anywhere; the same bare pair on an English keynote (detected language: en) → target 'zh'.
